/T (A function is a named block that does one job; breaking a program into functions makes it easier to test and reuse. C passes arguments by value \(a copy\). To let a function change the caller's data, or avoid copying a big structure, the address is passed as a pointer; const promises the function will not modify the data.)
/E (A function is a named block that does one job; breaking a program into functions makes it easier to test and reuse. C passes arguments by value \(a copy\). To let a function change the caller's data, or avoid copying a big structure, the address is passed as a pointer; const promises the function will not modify the data.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
