/T (Fixed +10 growth instead of doubling: for n donors, doubling copies at most about 2n elements in total, O\(n\) \(for 50,000 donors: 2 + 4 + ... + 32,768 = 65,534 copies\), but can leave up to about n/2 to n slots unused \(65,536 - 50,000 = 15,536 here\). Growing by 10 copies 10 + 20 + ... = about n2/20 elements, O\(n2\) \(about 125 million copies for 50,000 donors\), but wastes at most 9 slots.)
/E (Fixed +10 growth instead of doubling: for n donors, doubling copies at most about 2n elements in total, O\(n\) \(for 50,000 donors: 2 + 4 + ... + 32,768 = 65,534 copies\), but can leave up to about n/2 to n slots unused \(65,536 - 50,000 = 15,536 here\). Growing by 10 copies 10 + 20 + ... = about n2/20 elements, O\(n2\) \(about 125 million copies for 50,000 donors\), but wastes at most 9 slots.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [46 49 47 50 48]
>>
endobj
704 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 111 0 R
/T (Production decision \(B4.3\). For a nightly report \(one pass over the data\) the batch method is the better choice: it took almost the same time as loading everything \(627 ms vs 671 ms for 5 million donors\) but needed 0.15 MB instead of up to 128 MB. For interactive searches \(find by group, update\) the donors of one hospital \(about 1 MB for 50,000 donors\) can simply be kept in the growable list. If a single donor must be looked up by ID without loading everything, a separate sorted index file with fseek and binary search needs O\(1\) memory and O\(log n\) file reads \(about 23 reads for 5 million donors\) per lookup, compared with O\(1\) lookup but O\(n\) memory after loading.)
/E (Production decision \(B4.3\). For a nightly report \(one pass over the data\) the batch method is the better choice: it took almost the same time as loading everything \(627 ms vs 671 ms for 5 million donors\) but needed 0.15 MB instead of up to 128 MB. For interactive searches \(find by group, update\) the donors of one hospital \(about 1 MB for 50,000 donors\) can simply be kept in the growable list. If a single donor must be looked up by ID without loading everything, a separate sorted index file with fseek and binary search needs O\(1\) memory and O\(log n\) file reads \(about 23 reads for 5 million donors\) per lookup, compared with O\(1\) lookup but O\(n\) memory after loading.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [51]
