893 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 115 0 R
/T (Why updating record 9 fails and the file is not damaged: the file holds only records 0 to 4. fseek beyond the end is allowed, but fread then returns 0, so update_units returns 0 before any fwrite is made. Nothing is written, so the file stays exactly as it was \(output 1:40 2:25 3:12 4:20 5:30, only record 3 changed\).)
/E (Why updating record 9 fails and the file is not damaged: the file holds only records 0 to 4. fseek beyond the end is allowed, but fread then returns 0, so update_units returns 0 before any fwrite is made. Nothing is written, so the file stays exactly as it was \(output 1:40 2:25 3:12 4:20 5:30, only record 3 changed\).)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [42]
>>
endobj
894 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 115 0 R
/T (Observations. Both formats gave identical data on the 10-record test. Here the binary file is bigger \(36 bytes per record because name[24] is always stored in full, against about 23 bytes per text line\), yet it is roughly 10 to 15 times faster to read and write because there is no number-to-text conversion. Updating the 50,000th record took about 0.01 ms with fseek \(O\(1\)\) against about 21 ms for rewriting the text file \(O\(n\)\), roughly 1,000 times slower. Times vary slightly from run to run and between computers.)
/E (Observations. Both formats gave identical data on the 10-record test. Here the binary file is bigger \(36 bytes per record because name[24] is always stored in full, against about 23 bytes per text line\), yet it is roughly 10 to 15 times faster to read and write because there is no number-to-text conversion. Updating the 50,000th record took about 0.01 ms with fseek \(O\(1\)\) against about 21 ms for rewriting the text file \(O\(n\)\), roughly 1,000 times slower. Times vary slightly from run to run and between computers.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [43]
>>
endobj
895 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 115 0 R
/T (Engineering trade-off. Binary files are not always portable: another computer or compiler may use a different int size, byte order \(endianness\) or structure padding, so the same bytes can be read wrongly. Text is portable and human-readable but slower and not updatable in place. Recommendation: use a text \(CSV-style\) file for donors \(variable length, shared between hospitals, easy to check\) and a fixed-record binary file for stock \(numbers only, small, updated very often\).)
/E (Engineering trade-off. Binary files are not always portable: another computer or compiler may use a different int size, byte order \(endianness\) or structure padding, so the same bytes can be read wrongly. Text is portable and human-readable but slower and not updatable in place. Recommendation: use a text \(CSV-style\) file for donors \(variable length, shared between hospitals, easy to check\) and a fixed-record binary file for stock \(numbers only, small, updated very often\).)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [44]
>>
endobj
896 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 115 0 R
/T (One fwrite for the whole array: it needs 1 library call instead of n calls and the data must first be held in an array of n x sizeof\(record\) bytes \(for 8 stock records this is only 64 bytes\). Time stays O\(n\) because the same bytes are written, but with less call overhead; for huge files the memory cost of holding the whole array is the limit.)
/E (One fwrite for the whole array: it needs 1 library call instead of n calls and the data must first be held in an array of n x sizeof\(record\) bytes \(for 8 stock records this is only 64 bytes\). Time stays O\(n\) because the same bytes are written, but with less call overhead; for huge files the memory cost of holding the whole array is the limit.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [45]
>>
endobj
