<<
/Title (Observations. Both formats gave identical data on the 10-record test. Here the binary file is bigger \(36 bytes per record because name[24] is always stored in full, against about 23 bytes per text line\), yet it is roughly 10 to 15 times faster to read and write because there is no number-to-text conversion. Updating the 50,000th record took about 0.01 ms with fseek \(O\(1\)\) against about 21 ms for rewriting the text file \(O\(n\)\), roughly 1,000 times slower. Times vary slightly from run to run and between computers.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1949 0 R
/Prev 1959 0 R
/Next 1981 0 R
>>
endobj
1972 0 obj
<<
/Title (One fwrite for the whole array: it needs 1 library call instead of n calls and the data must first be held in an array of n x sizeof\(record\) bytes \(for 8 stock records this is only 64 bytes\). Time stays O\(n\) because the same bytes are written, but with less call overhead; for huge files the memory cost of holding the whole array is the limit.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1949 0 R
/Prev 1981 0 R
/Next 1960 0 R
>>
endobj
