2072 0 obj
<<
/Title (Changing half * half to two calls gives 63 calls for n = 30 instead of 6, and 32,767 calls for n = 10,000. The same sub-problem is solved twice at every level, so T\(n\) = 2T\(n/2\) + 1 = O\(n\) and the speed advantage of halving is lost. Recursion limit: the linear version crashed between n = 261,742 and 262,505 on this machine. With an 8 MB stack and about 32 bytes per frame, 8,388,608 / 32 = 262,144 frames, which agrees. The crash is a stack overflow \(segmentation fault\). The iterative version handled n = 1,000,000,000 using constant stack. The limit depends on the machine and stack size, so rerun stack_limit on your own computer.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2054 0 R
/Prev 2082 0 R
/Next 2061 0 R
>>
endobj
2073 0 obj
<<
/Title (Both recursive functions worked and matched the iterative and formula versions on all tests. The call counts \(31 vs 6 for 2^30\) matched the recurrences, and the linear version overflowed the stack near n = 262,000. I learned that a base case and a small recursion depth are essential.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2062 0 R
/First 2083 0 R
/Last 2083 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
